  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Examining Design & Implementation**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of design and implementation. I\'m focusing on creating an interactive background component, and figuring out the best way to do this using React or HTML5 Canvas with Vanilla JS. I\'m considering which framework will be most efficient and versatile for this project.\n\n\n**Analyzing Visual Aesthetic**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the visual aesthetic, aiming for a smooth, velvety canvas background in a deep midnight navy. My goal is a soft, volumetric \"light beam\" originating from the top-right and projecting diagonally. The core colors will be electric cyan blending into neon purple, with a soft, diffused radial blur to create an atmospheric feel, using mix-blend-mode: screen. No harsh lines allowed!\n\n\n**Designing Interactive Elements**\n\nI\'m now designing the interactive elements. I\'m focusing on incorporating gentle idle motion into the light beam, achieved with subtle scale or opacity shifts using CSS keyframes or requestAnimationFrame. I plan to make the beam react to cursor movement by implementing a gentle parallax effect. I also need to design an interactive toggle switch for the light beam. It should have a minimalist glassmorphic style and allow users to switch between the dark cosmic beam and an illuminated state by updating CSS custom variables.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Volumetric Rendering**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the volumetric rendering. I\'ve broken down how to render the volumetric beam. I\'m choosing between HTML5 Canvas and WebGL. I\'m now leaning toward the HTML5 2D Canvas route because it enables sharp, resolution-independent rendering. It is also capable of high framerates with mouse interpolation and zero grainy artifacts. Using a HTML5 Canvas rendering loop calculates the cone geometry. It calculates multiple feathered radial/conical gradients.\n\n\n**Refining Layered Effects**\n\nI\'m now refining the layered effects, considering either CSS backdrop filters or a Canvas 2D render. I\'m structuring a vibrant beam with electric cyan blending to neon violet. I\'m also exploring atmospheric cones and ambient glows, incorporating a breathing wave effect and cursor-dampened motion. I have a clear vision for the origin point as well.\n\n\n**Visualizing Illumination Transformations**\n\nI\'m now visualizing the illumination transformations based on the \"Illuminate\" toggle. In dark mode, it\'s the neon cyan/purple volumetric glow. However, when toggled, I\'m imagining the toggle illuminating and the beam blossoming with either warm golden light, aurora, or electric radiance. I am now adding mode selector options like \"Cosmic Neon\", \"Aurora Borealis\", \"Solar Flare\", and \"Moonlight Silver\", plus controls. I will make this both an interactive backdrop and provide a developer drawer.\n\n\n**Enforcing Negative Constraints**\n\nI\'m now strictly enforcing the negative constraints, ensuring there are absolutely no visual elements like grids, dots, or particles. The focus is on a completely smooth, velvety background. I\'ve also re-examined the \"Illuminate\" toggle, confirming its minimalist, glassmorphic design and its ability to smoothly update CSS custom variables upon interaction. The toggle will be fixed in the top right.\n\n\n**Refining Beam Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now refining the beam dynamics further. I\'m focusing on the toggle\'s design, thinking about a glassmorphic pill switch with a sliding thumb. The switch will have a glowing LED indicator or lamp bulb graphic physically connecting to the light beam\'s origin, which is top-right (e.g. `top-8 right-8` or `top-6 right-8`). When on, the beam\'s intensity and diffusion will increase, transitioning CSS custom properties smoothly. In the off-state, it will cut through the dark with velvety depth.\n\n\n**Calculating Beam Physics**\n\nI\'m now calculating the beam physics. I\'ve defined a base angle of approximately 220 degrees for the beam\'s direction. I\'m incorporating parallax mouse influence, calculating delta angle based on mouse position, using smooth damping for a natural feel. I\'m also implementing breathing motion with subtle rotation sway and intensity pulse, along with a breathing spread. I am choosing between multiple methods, A and B, to draw the volumetric cone on canvas.\n\n\n**Constructing Gradients & Feathering**\n\nI\'m now constructing the radial gradients for the beam, utilizing specific color stops to achieve the desired effect. I\'m focusing on an electric cyan core, transitioning to neon purple and dissolving into the dark background. I\'m exploring different methods to feather the edges, including multiple passes with decreasing opacity, and hardware-accelerated blur filters. Layering looks like the best approach: a broad ambient diffusion with a wide angle and soft haze. I\'m finding the hardware blur is smooth.\n\n\n**Refining Rendering Passes**\n\nI\'m now refining the layered rendering passes for the volumetric beam. I\'m focusing on the rendering passes that are needed. I\'m now establishing four separate passes. Pass 2 focuses on the main volumetric body, with a medium angle and electric cyan blending into neon violet, using screen blending. Pass 3 will focus on a core, high-intensity beam with a tighter angle. Pass 4 will focus on the origin point aperture glow, to give the feeling of the beam originating from the switch. Pass 5 adds a light bounce wash in the bottom-left corner. Using `ctx.filter = \'blur(...)\'` is working really well.\n\n\n**Confirming Browser Compatibility**\n\nI\'m now confirming comprehensive browser compatibility. All modern browsers fully support `ctx.filter = \'blur(...px)\'`, so the main rendering approach is now secure. I\'ve confirmed fallback options using `backdrop-filter` or SVG Gaussian blur for older browsers. The user will be able to interact with and toggle the background, giving me the freedom to add controls and features, such as smooth parallax tracking, gentle idle breathing, and an interactive origin point.\n\n\n**Implementing Customization Controls**\n\nI\'m now implementing interactive customization controls. I\'ve placed the glassmorphic toggle in the top-right corner, and now I\'m working on the design of the pill bar or drawer. It will have several color mode options: \"Cosmic Neon,\" \"Ultraviolet Dream,\" \"Cyber Emerald,\" \"Solar Dawn,\" and \"Pure Moonlight.\" Each mode alters the beam colors when toggled.\n\n\n**Refining Customization Controls**\n\nI\'m now fine-tuning the sliders for the customization controls within the pill bar. I am adding controls for beam intensity, width/spread, sway sensitivity, and breathing speed with options to reset. I\'m focusing on the user experience and ensuring these controls are subtly tuckable. I am developing minimalist hero content to demonstrate the background\'s visual impact, including a \"Copy React Component\" button, a \"Hide UI\" toggle, and sleek typography. I am making sure the layout adheres to design guidelines.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_1453695"
      name: "replace_file_content"
      arguments_json: "{\"AllowMultiple\":false,\"Description\":\"Update metadata.json with title and description for the Volumetric Light Beam applet\",\"EndLine\":7,\"Instruction\":\"Set applet name and description\",\"ReplacementContent\":\"{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"Volumetric Light Beam\\\",\\n  \\\"description\\\": \\\"Interactive, velvety smooth volumetric light beam canvas background with mouse reactivity and glassmorphic Illuminate toggle.\\\",\\n  \\\"requestFramePermissions\\\": [],\\n  \\\"majorCapabilities\\\": [\\\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\\\"]\\n}\\n\",\"StartLine\":1,\"TargetContent\":\"{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"description\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"requestFramePermissions\\\": [],\\n  \\\"majorCapabilities\\\": [\\\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\\\"]\\n}\",\"TargetFile\":\"/metadata.json\",\"toolAction\":\"Updating metadata.json\",\"toolSummary\":\"Set applet title and description\"}"
