  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Cursor Behavior**\n\nI\'m removing the cursor tracking listener entirely to lock the light beam to a fixed diagonal angle \342\200\223 top-right to bottom-left. This streamlines the visual experience and eliminates the need for any on-screen movement to adjust the beam. This simplifies the user experience significantly.\n\n\n**Implementing Visual Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now incorporating visual enhancements. I\'m adding scattered, twinkling star particles that float upwards, catching the light beam and receiving a cyan glint when they intersect. The light beam is locked to a diagonal angle, moving to a subtle breathing animation to create a less active experience, all while removing the pointer instructions. I\'ve begun to review the `src/components/VolumetricLightBeam.tsx` file for modification.\n\n\n**Adding Particle Interaction**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the particle system. I\'m aiming for an organic distribution of around 70-110 particles, each with properties for position, radius, and drift speed. I\'m working on calculating the angle between each particle and the light beam to determine when they intersect and receive the cyan glint. I\'ve been reviewing the `src/components/VolumetricLightBeam.tsx` file for modification.\n\n\n**Optimizing Particle Behavior**\n\nI\'m now refining particle interaction within the light beam. Based on distance and proximity to the beam\'s center, I\'m calculating an illumination factor. Inside the beam, particles brighten, with an increased alpha and a soft electric cyan or neon purple glint, alongside a faint halo. Outside the beam, they remain faint white/silver. These particles will float upwards, drifting ambiently. I\'m focusing on ensuring smooth 60fps performance and seamless particle wrapping. I\'m also removing cursor sway references in `src/App.tsx`.\n\n\n"
